  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 940 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nRidging builds in over the Intermountain West Sunday as the Southern \nPlains high begins drifting westward. As a result, dry air filters \nin across the region, a trend which continues through Monday night. \nPrecipitation chances will be near-zero, although a low probability \n(less than 15%) of a stray shower or thunderstorm does remain over \nthe higher terrain the next few days. Temperatures will run near-\nnormal through Monday.\n\nA weak frontal boundary sets up across the Northern Great Basin \nTuesday into Wednesday, tightening up the surface pressure gradient \nacross northeast Utah and northwest Colorado. Some gustier winds of \n20-30 mph will be possible in this region, although surface relative \nhumidity values will be high enough that critical fire weather \nconditions are not anticipated. Some extra moisture accompanies this \nfrontal boundary, with PWAT values forecast to be 100-150% of normal \nacross the region, with the best moisture north of I-70. With a \ncombination of orographics and a bit of forcing from the frontal \nboundary, some isolated to widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will be possible across the higher terrain Tuesday and \nWednesday afternoon. Slightly above normal moisture lingers through \nthe end of the work week, leading to lower-end (20-30%) chances of \nshowers or thunderstorms over the higher terrain each afternoon. The \nmain threats with these storms will be gusty outflow winds and \nlightning, although a brief heavy downpour cannot be ruled out. \n\nHigh pressure settles over the Four Corners Thursday into Friday, \nushering in a drying and warming trend. By the end of the week, high \ntemperatures are expected to run around 10 degrees above normal, \nwith triple digits across the lower desert valleys and the Four \nCorners. Friday into the weekend, models are indicating a trough \nmoving into the Pacific Northwest will nudge the high eastward once \nagain, returning weak monsoonal flow into the Four Corners.",
